comments.content:
#### Type Narrowing Issue in Error Handler

The error handler attempts to access `err.errors` when `err.name === "ZodError"`, but `err` is typed as `Error | AppError`, and neither type guarantees the existence of the `errors` property. This will cause a TypeScript error in strict mode and may lead to runtime issues.

**Recommended Solution:**
Use a type assertion or proper type narrowing to safely access `err.errors`. For example:

```typescript
if (err.name === "ZodError" && (err as any).errors) {
  return res.status(400).json({
    error: "Validation failed",
    details: (err as any).errors.map((e: any) => `${e.path.join(".")}: ${e.message}`),
    requestId
  });
}
```
Alternatively, use a ZodError type guard if available.

